long the $x$-axis over the interval $1 \\le x \\le 2$, where $x$ is measured in meters. Find the moment of the rod about the origin."] [17592186198565 :question/answer-fields 17592186194291] [17592186198567 :question/problem "Consider a thin rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $0 \\le x \\le 2$ whose mass density function is $λ(x) = 3x$. Given that the rod's mass is $6$ units, what is the $x$-coordinate of its center of mass?"] [17592186197980 :question/problem "The thin rod below has the mass density function $\\lambda(x) =2x\\, \\textrm{kg/m}$, where $x$ is measured in meters. What is the total mass of the rod?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4167/Source/Images/ab9b1a56295dd675dc0079231bebfe76.png)"] [17592186197980 :question/worked-solution "Given a thin rod oriented along the $x$-axis over the interval\n\n$$\na \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nif $\\lambda(x)$ denotes the mass density function of the rod, then the total mass of the rod is given by\n\n$$\nm = \\int_a^b \\lambda(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x) = 2x, \\qquad a=1, \\qquad b=3\n$$\n\nWe calculate the mass of the rod as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= ∫_{1}^{3}2xdx \\\\\n&= [x^{2}]_{1}^{3} \\\\\n&= 3^{2} - 1^{2} \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the rod is $8\\,\\textrm{kg}$."] [17592186197978 :question/problem "The thin rod below has the mass density function $\\lambda (x) = (x+4)\\,\\textrm{kg/m}$. Find the moment of the rod about the origin.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4167/Source/Images/ac2d4ddc8a114f8f9399775fec32db1a.png)"] [17592186197978 :question/worked-solution "The moment about the origin $O$ of a rod with mass density function $\\lambda(x)$ for\n\n$$\na \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\nM_O = \\int_a^b x\\, \\lambda(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x) = x+4, \\qquad a=0, \\qquad b=3\n$$\n\nWe calculate the moment of the rod about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{O} &= ∫_{0}^{3}x \\cdot (x + 4)dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{3}x^{2} + 4xdx \\\\\n&= [\\frac{x^{3}}{3} + 2x^{2}]_{0}^{3} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{3^{3}}{3} + 2 \\cdot 3^{2}) - 0 \\\\\n&= 27\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required moment is $27\\,\\textrm{kg}\\cdot \\textrm{m}$."] [17592186197979 :question/problem "The thin rod above has the mass density function $\\lambda (x) = x^3$. Given that the rod's mass is $4$ units, what is the $x$-coordinate of its center of mass?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4167/Source/Images/8c63fd7d44dfaf371d96c176253bfc52.png)"] [17592186197979 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval\n\n$$\na \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nwhose mass density function is $\\lambda(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{M_O}{m}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\displaystyle m = \\int_a^b \\lambda (x) \\: \\textrm{d}x$ is the mass of the rod,\n- $\\displaystyle M_O = \\int_a^b \\, x\\lambda (x) \\: \\textrm{d}x$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O$.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x) = x^3, \\qquad a=0, \\qquad b=2\n$$\n\nSo, the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{O} &= ∫_{0}^{2}x \\cdot x^{3}dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}x^{4}dx \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{5}x^{5}]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}(32 - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{32}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}= \\dfrac{M_O}{m} = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{32}{5}\\right)}{4} = \\dfrac{8}{5}\n$$\n\nNote that this result makes intuitive sense. Since $\\lambda$ is an increasing function of $x$, we would expect the center of mass to be located closer to the right endpoint and further from the left endpoint."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186194292 17592186194293 17592186194294 17592186194295 17592186194296 17592186194304 17592186194305 17592186194306 17592186194307 17592186194308] "immutable_fields" [[17592186194291 :answer-field/correct] [17592186194291 :answer-field/id] [17592186194291 :answ